      "text": "THE RITE OF SPRING (1913):\n  Choreography: Vaclav Nijinsky\n  Music: Igor Stravinsky\n  Premiere: May 29, 1913, Paris — RIOT in the theater.\n  The story: the chosen maiden dances herself to death\n  in a pagan ritual.\n  The music: rhythmically violent, harmonically brutal,\n  no conventional melody. The famous 34-note rhythmic\n  irregularity in the \"Augurs of Spring.\"\n  The choreography: turned-IN feet (opposite of classical\n  ballet); hunched posture; angular, jerky movement.\n  Everything that classical ballet prized, violated.\n  The riot: legend (probably both pro and con factions\n  in the audience; the accounts vary). Authentic: the\n  work was genuinely disorienting.\n  Now recognized as the most important musical event\n  of the 20th century (with Schonberg's op. 11 Pieces\n  as the other candidate)."
